Served as a Lab Instructor, collaborating with a teaching team to suport a climate science course targeted toward non-STEM students

<a href='https://registrar.princeton.edu/course-offerings/course-details?courseid=011153&term=1272'>Climate: Past, Present, and Future (GEO-102)</a>

## Responsibilities included:
* Coordinating with faculty, course manager, and 4 other TAs to ensure content was presented consistently across labs and lecture for a 150+ student course
* Guiding 10-15 students through weekly 3-hour labs using hands-on demonstrations of course concepts and developing skills in experimental design, data collection/analysis, and scientific communication
* Synthesizing and communicating student feedback to faculty and course manager, addressing student concerns and more effectively achieving faculty goals
* Assisting in the review and development of lab materials, problem sets, and exams
* Grading and providing feedback on labs, exams, and homework assignments
* Hosting weekly office hours and additional supplemental meetings for undergraduate students

## Course description:
Are human activities changing our climate? Is climate change a major problem? To answer these questions, we will learn the basics of how Earth's climate works and how climate shapes environments. We will also investigate past climate changes and identify the most important ways in which climate change can affect the natural world and humans. Finally, we will use climate science to evaluate possible responses and solutions. The course is designed for students from all majors--no science background required--while still offering a comprehensive overview for students seeking to pursue climate-related issues.

